HTTP Rich-Context Approach (Recommended for AppId-only or AppId+Secret)
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Concept
- Dataplane accepts Bearer JWT or app credentials.
- For app credentials, dataplane calls new controlplane endpoint `/iudx/auth/v2/app/context` over HTTPS with its own service credential.
- Controlplane returns a full authorization context (AppAuthContext) containing all fields downstream handlers need (roles, constraints, iid, allowedAttributes, accessPolicy, ownerUserId, expiry metadata).
- Dataplane injects this context into RoutingContext keys already used by downstream handlers, so business logic remains unchanged.

Flow (app credential path)
1. Client sends headers: `appId` (and optionally `appSecret` if policy requires).
2. AuthenticationHandler detects absence of Bearer token, presence of appId (and secret if mandated).
3. Dataplane -> Controlplane: POST `/iudx/auth/v2/app/context` with JSON `{ appId, appSecret? }`, plus `Authorization: Bearer <dataplane-service-token>` or mTLS client cert.
4. Controlplane validates app, secret (if any), status, revocation, constraints; returns JSON payload (AppAuthContext).
5. AuthenticationHandler maps fields into RoutingContext:
   - iid
   - role / delegated role
   - cons / allowedAttributes / accessPolicy
   - ownerUserId
   - expiry / cache-control version
6. Downstream handlers proceed exactly as in JWT branch (no code change).

Data contract: AppAuthContext (example fields)
{
  "appId": "uuid",
  "role": "consumer|provider|delegate|org_admin|compute",
  "iid": "provider:resourceId" | null,
  "cons": { "allowedAttributes": [...], "access": "...", "constraintsVersion": "v123" },
  "allowedAttributes": [...],
  "accessPolicy": "OPEN|RESTRICTED|DENY",
  "ownerUserId": "uuid",
  "drl": "delegated-role?" ,
  "did": "delegator-id?",
  "expiresAt": 1712345678,
  "revocationVersion": 42
}

Handler updates
- New AuthenticationHandler (first in chain) branches JWT vs app.
- ApiConstants: add `appId`, `appSecret` header constants; allow in CORS.
- Main verticle: inject controlplane base URL, service credential, timeouts, retry policy.
- Auditing: log auth method and appId.

Service-to-service auth
- Prefer mTLS between dataplane and controlplane.
- If mTLS not ready, issue a long-lived dataplane service JWT scoped to the app-context endpoint.

Caching
- Positive cache TTL: 30–60 seconds, keyed by appId (+secret hash if used) and revocationVersion.
- Negative cache TTL: 5–10 seconds to avoid thundering herd.
- Fail-closed on controlplane errors by default; make fail-open a feature flag for read-only endpoints if required.

Pros
- Single controlplane call; no JWT minting overhead.
- Works when clients send only appId.
- Instant revocation via revocationVersion.

Cons
- Requires new controlplane endpoint and schema.
- Dataplane must manage dual auth contexts (JWT vs AppAuthContext).

Rollout steps
1) Add controlplane endpoint and service credential.
2) Implement AuthenticationHandler branch and mapping.
3) Wire CORS/OpenAPI/headers.
4) Add metrics: latency of app-context calls, cache hit ratio, auth-method split.
5) Gradual enablement behind feature flag per route.

